"Internal Error ()\nciteturn97view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S1\",\"lineno\":319}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n6 Black Holes‣ General Relativity by David Tong (https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S6)\nciteturn97view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/grhtml/S6\",\"lineno\":140}); Total lines: 1068\nL68: ### 6.1.1 Birkhoff’s Theorem\nL69: \nL71: ####\nL72: \nL73: Here we provide a sketch of the proof. The first half of the proof involves setting up a useful set of coordinates. First, we make use of the statement that the metric is spherically symmetric, which means that it has an SO(3) isometry. One of the more fiddly parts of the proof is to show that any metric with such an isometry can be written in coordinates that make this isometry manifest,\nL74:  | d{s}^{2}={g}_{\\tau \\tau }(\\tau ,\\rho )d{\\tau }^{2}+2{g}_{\\tau \\rho }(\\tau ,\\rho )d\\tau d\\rho +{g}_{\\rho \\rho }(\\tau ,\\rho )d{\\rho }^{2}+{r}^{2}(\\tau ,\\rho )d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |\nL75: \nL76: Here \\tau and \\rho are some coordinates and d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2} is the familiar metric on {𝐒}^{2}\nL77: \nL78:  | d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}=d{\\theta }^{2}+{\\mathrm{sin}}^{2}\\theta d{\\varphi }^{2}  |\nL79: The SO(3) isometry then acts on this {𝐒}^{2} in the usual way, leaving \\tau and \\rho untouched. This is said to be a foliation of the space by the spheres {𝐒}^{2}.\nL80: ####\nL81: \nL82: The size of the sphere is determined by the function r(\\tau ,\\rho ) in the above metric. The next step in the proof is to change coordinates so that we work with \\tau and r, rather than \\tau and \\rho . We’re then left with the metric\nL83: \nL84:  | d{s}^{2}={g}_{\\tau \\tau }(\\tau ,r)d{\\tau }^{2}+2{g}_{\\tau r}(\\tau ,r)d\\tau dr+{g}_{rr}(\\tau ,r)d{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |\nL85: In fact there’s a subtlety in the argument above: for some functions r(\\tau ,\\rho ), it’s not possible to exchange \\rho for r. Examples of such functions include r=\\mathrm{constant} and r=\\tau . We can rule out such counter-examples by insisting that asymptotically the spacetime looks like Minkowski space.\nL86: ####\nL87: \nL88: Our next step is to introduce a new coordinate that gets rid of the cross-term {g}_{\\tau r}. To this end, consider the a coordinate \\stackrel{~}{t}(\\tau ,r). Then\nL89:  | d{\\stackrel{~}{t}}^{2}={\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial \\stackrel{~}{t}}{\\partial \\tau }}\\right)}^{2}d{\\tau }^{2}+2{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial \\stackrel{~}{t}}{\\partial \\tau }}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial \\stackrel{~}{t}}{\\partial r}}d\\tau dr+{\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial \\stackrel{~}{t}}{\\partial r}}\\right)}^{2}d{r}^{2}  |\nL90: We can always pick a choice of \\stackrel{~}{t}(\\tau ,r) so that the cross-term {g}_{\\tau r} vanishes in the new coordinates. We’re then left with the simpler looking metric,\nL91: \nL92:  | d{s}^{2}=-f(\\stackrel{~}{t},r)d{\\stackrel{~}{t}}^{2}+g(\\stackrel{~}{t},r)d{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |\nL93: Where we’ve now included the expected minus sign in the temporal part of the metric, reflecting our chosen signature. This is as far as we can go making useful coordinate choices. To proceed, we need to use the Einstein equations. As always, this involves sitting down and doing a fiddly calculation. Here we present only the (somewhat surprising) conclusion: the vacuum Einstein equations require that\nL94:  | f(r,\\stackrel{~}{t})=f(r)h(\\stackrel{~}{t})\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\mathrm{and}\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }g(r,\\stackrel{~}{t})=g(r)  |\nL95: \nL96: In other words, the metric takes the form\nL97: \nL98:  | d{s}^{2}=-f(r)h(\\stackrel{~}{t})d{\\stackrel{~}{t}}^{2}+g(r)d{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |\nL99: \nL100: But we can always absorb that h(\\stackrel{~}{t}) factor by redefining the time coordinate, so that h(\\stackrel{~}{t})d{\\stackrel{~}{t}}^{2}=d{t}^{2}. Finally, we’re left with the a metric of the form\nL101:  | d{s}^{2}=-f(r)d{t}^{2}+g(r)d{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |  | (6.257)\nL102: This is important. We assumed that the metric was spherically symmetric, but made no such assumption about the lack of time dependence. Yet the Einstein equations have forced this upon us, and the final metric (cite35†6.257 ) has two sets of Killing vectors. The first arises from the SO(3) isometry that we originally assumed, but the second is the timelike Killing vector K={\\partial }_{t} that has emerged from the calculation.\nL103: ####\nL104: \nL105: At this point we need to finish solving the Einstein equations. It turns out that they require f(r)=g{(r)}^{-1}, so the metric (cite35†6.257 ) reduces to the simple ansatz (cite27†4.155 ) that we considered previously. The Schwarzschild solution (cite34†6.256 ) is the most general solution to the Einstein equations with vanishing cosmological constant.\nL106: ####\nL107: \nL108: The fact that we assumed only spherical symmetry, and not time independence, means that the Schwarzschild solution not only describes the spacetime outside a time-independent star, but also outside a collapsing star, providing that the collapse is spherically symmetric.\nL109: \nL110: ### A Closer Look at Time Independence\nL111: \nL112: There are actually two, different meanings to “time independence” in general relativity.\nL113: ####\nL114: \nL115: A spacetime is said to be stationary if it admits an everywhere timelike Kililng vector field K. In asymptotically flat spacetimes, we usually normalise this so that {K}^{2}\\to -1 asymptotically.\nL116: \nL117: ####\nL118: \nL119: A spacetime is said to be static if it is stationary and, in addition, is invariant under t\\to -t, where t is a coordinate along the integral curves of K. In particular, this rules out dtdX cross-terms in the metric, with X some other coordinate.\nL120: ####\nL121: \nL122: Birkhoff’s theorem tells us that spherical symmetry implies that the spacetime is necessarily static. In Section cite20†6.3 , we’ll come across spacetimes that are stationary but not static.\nL123: ### 6.1.2 A First Look at the Horizon\nL124: There are two values of r where the Schwarzschild metric goes bad: r=0 and r=2GM. At each of these values, one of the components of the metric diverges but, as we will see, the interpretation of this divergence is rather different in the two cases. We will learn that the divergence at the point r=0 is because the spacetime is sick: this point is called the singularity.\nL125: The theory of general relativity breaks down as we get close to the singularity and to make sense of what’s happening there we need to turn to a quantum theory of spacetime.\nL126: ####\nL127: \nL128: In contrast, nothing so dramatic happens at the surface r=2GM and the divergence in the metric is merely because we’ve made a poor choice of coordinates: this surface is referred to as the event horizon, usually called simply the horizon. Many of the surprising properties of black holes lie in interpreting the event horizon.\nL129: ####\nL130: There is a simple diagnostic to determine whether a divergence in the metric is due to a true singularity of the spacetime, or to a poor choice of coordinates. We build a scalar quantity that does not depend on the choice of coordinates. If this too diverges then it’s telling us that the spacetime itself is indeed sick at that point. If it does not diverge, we can’t necessarily conclude that the spacetime isn’t sick because there may be some other scalar quantity that signifies there is a problem.\nL131: Nonetheless, we might start to wonder if perhaps nothing very bad happens.\nL132: ####\nL133: \nL134: The simplest scalar is, of course, the Ricci scalar. But this is necessarily R=0 for any vacuum solution to the Einstein equation, so is not helpful in detecting the nature of singularities. The same is true for {R}_{\\mu \\nu }{R}^{\\mu \\nu }. For this reason, the simplest curvature diagnostic is the Kretschmann scalar, {R}^{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }{R}_{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }. For the Schwarzschild solution it is given by\nL135:  | {R}^{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }{R}_{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }={\\displaystyle \\frac{48{G}^{2}{M}^{2}}{{r}^{6}}}  |  | (6.258)\nL136: We see that the Kretschmann scalar exhibits no pathology at the surface r=2GM, where {R}^{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }{R}_{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }\\sim 1/{(GM)}^{4}. This suggests that perhaps this divergence in the metric isn’t as worrisome as it may have first appeared. Note moreover that, perhaps counter-intuitively, heavier black holes have smaller curvature at the horizon. We see that this arises because such black holes are bigger and the 1/{r}^{6} factor beats the {M}^{2} factor.\nL137: ####\nL138: \nL139: In contrast, the curvature indeed diverges at the origin r=0, telling us that the spacetime is problematic at this point. Of course, given that we have still to understand the horizon at r=2GM, it’s not entirely clear that we can trust the Schwarzschild metric for values r<2GM. As we will proceed, we will see that the singularity at r=0 is a genuine feature of the (classical) black hole.\nL140: ### The Near Horizon Limit: Rindler Space\nL141: \nL142: To understand what’s happening near the horizon r=2GM, we can zoom in and look at the metric in the vicinity of the horizon. To do this, we write\nL143: \nL144:  | r=2GM+\\eta  |\nL145: \nL146: where we take \\eta \\ll 2GM. We further take \\eta >0 which means that we’re looking at the region of spacetime just outside the horizon. We then approximate the components of the metric as\nL147:  | 1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\approx {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\eta }{2GM}}\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\mathrm{and}\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }{r}^{2}={(2GM+\\eta )}^{2}\\approx {(2GM)}^{2}  |\nL148: \nL149: To this order, the Schwarzschild metric becomes\nL150: \nL151:  | d{s}^{2}=-{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\eta }{2GM}}d{t}^{2}+{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{\\eta }}d{\\eta }^{2}+{(2GM)}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |\nL152: The first thing that we see is that the metric has decomposed into a direct product of an {𝐒}^{2} of radius 2GM, and a d=1+1 dimensional Lorentzian geometry. We’ll focus on this 2d Lorentzian geometry. We make the change of variables\nL153: \nL154:  | {\\rho }^{2}=8GM\\eta  |\nL155: \nL156: after which the 2d metric becomes\nL157: \nL158:  | d{s}^{2}=-{\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{\\rho }{4GM}}\\right)}^{2}d{t}^{2}+d{\\rho }^{2}  |\nL159: This rather simple metric is known as Rindler space. It is, in fact, just Minkowski space in disguise. The disguise is the transformation\nL160: \nL161:  | T=\\rho \\mathrm{sinh}\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{t}{4GM}}\\right)\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\mathrm{and}\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }X=\\rho \\mathrm{cosh}\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{t}{4GM}}\\right)  |  | (6.259)\nL162: \nL163: after which the metric becomes\nL164: \nL165:  | d{s}^{2}=-d{T}^{2}+d{X}^{2}  |  | (6.260)\nL166: We’ve met something very similar to the coordinates (cite36†6.259 ) previously: they are the coordinates experienced by an observer undergoing constant acceleration a=1/4GM, where t is the proper time of this observer. (We saw such coordinates earlier in (cite37†1.26 ) which differ only by a constant offset to the spatial variable \\rho .) This makes sense: an observer who sits at a constant \\rho value, corresponding to a constant r value, must accelerate in order to avoid falling into the black hole.\nL167: Figure 43: The near horizon limit of a black hole is Rindler spacetime, with the null lines X=\\pm T corresponding to the horizon at r=2GM. Also shown in red is a line of constant r>2GM outside the black hole.\nL168: ####\nL169: \nL170: We can now start to map out what part of Minkowski space (cite38†6.260 ) corresponds to the outside of the black hole horizon. This is \\rho >0 and t\\in (-\\mathrm{\\infty },+\\mathrm{\\infty }). From the change of variables (cite36†6.259 ), we see that this corresponds the region X>|T|.\nL171: ####\nL172: \nL173: We can also see what becomes of the horizon itself. This sits at r=2GM, or \\rho =0. For any finite t, the horizon \\rho =0 gets mapped to the origin of Minkowski space, X=T=0. However, the time coordinate is degenerate at the horizon since {g}_{00}=0. If we scale t\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }, and \\rho \\to 0 keeping the combination \\rho {e}^{\\pm t/4GM} fixed, then we see that the horizon actually corresponds to the lines:\nL174:  | r=2GM\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\Rightarrow \\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }X=\\pm T  |\nL175: \nL176: This is our first lesson. The event horizon of a black hole is not a timelike surface, like the surface of a star. Instead it is a null surface. This is depicted in Figure cite39†43 .\nL177: ####\nL178: Although our starting point was restricted to coordinates X and T given by (cite36†6.259 ), once we get to the Minkowski space metric (cite38†6.260 ) there’s no reason to retain this restriction. Indeed, clearly the metric makes perfect sense if we extend the range of the coordinates to X,T\\in 𝐑. Moreover, this metric makes it clear that nothing fishy is happening at the horizon X=\\pm |T|. We see that if we zoom in on the horizon, then it’s no different from any other part of spacetime.\nL179: Nonetheless, as we go on we will learn that the horizon does have some rather special properties, but you only get to see them if you look at things from a more global perspective.\nL180: ### 6.1.3 Eddington-Finkelstein Coordinates\nL181: \nL182: Above we saw that, in the near-horizon limit, a clever change of variables allows us to remove the coordinate singularity at the horizon and extend the spacetime beyond. Our goal in this section is to play the same game, but now for the full black hole metric.\nL183: ####\nL184: Before we proceed, it’s worth commenting on the logic here. When we first met differential geometry in Section cite3†2 , we made a big deal of the fact that a single set of coordinates need not cover the entire manifold. Instead, one typically needs different coordinates in different patches, together with transition functions that relate the coordinates where the patches overlap. The situation with the black hole is similar, but not quite the same.\nL185: It’s true that the coordinates of the Schwarzschild metric (cite34†6.256 ) do not cover the entire spacetime: they break down at r=2GM and it’s not clear that we should trust the metric for r<2GM. But rather than finding a new set of coordinates in the region beyond the horizon, and trying to patch this together with our old coordinates, we’re instead going to find a new set of coordinates that works everywhere.\nL186: ####\nL187: \nL188: Our first step is to introduce a new radial coordinate, {r}_{\\star }, defined by\nL189: \nL190:  | d{r}_{\\star }^{2}={\\left(1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\right)}^{-2}d{r}^{2}  |  | (6.261)\nL191: \nL192: The solution to this differential equation is straightforward to find: it is\nL193: \nL194:  | {r}_{\\star }=r+2GM\\mathrm{log}\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{r-2GM}{2GM}}\\right)  |  | (6.262)\nL195: We see that the region outside the horizon 2GM<r<\\mathrm{\\infty } maps to -\\mathrm{\\infty }<{r}_{\\star }<+\\mathrm{\\infty } in the new coordinate. As we approach the horizon, the change in r is increasingly slow as we vary {r}_{\\star } (since dr/d{r}_{\\star }\\to 0 as r\\to 2GM.) For this reason it is called the tortoise coordinate. (It is also sometimes called the Regge-Wheeler radial coordinate.)\nL196: ####\nL197: \nL198: The tortoise coordinate is well adapted to describe the path of light rays travelling in the radial direction. Such light rays follow curves satisfying\nL199: \nL200:  | d{s}^{2}=0\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\Rightarrow \\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }{\\displaystyle \\frac{dr}{dt}}=\\pm \\left(1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\right)\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\Rightarrow \\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }{\\displaystyle \\frac{d{r}_{\\star }}{dt}}=\\pm 1  |\nL201: \nL202: We see that null, radial geodesics are given by\nL203:  | t\\pm {r}_{\\star }=\\mathrm{constant}  |\nL204: \nL205: where the plus sign corresponds to ingoing geodesics (as t increases, {r}_{\\star } must decrease) and the negative sign to outgoing geodesics.\nL206: ####\nL207: \nL208: Next, we introduce a pair of null coordinates\nL209: \nL210:  | v=t+{r}_{\\star }\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }\\mathrm{and}\\mathit{\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em} }u=t-{r}_{\\star }  |\nL211: \nL212: In what follows we will consider the Schwarzschild metric written first in coordinates (v,r), then in coordinates (u,r) and finally, in Section cite11†6.1.4 , in coordinates (u,v).\nL213: ### Ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein Coordinates\nL214: \nL215: As a first attempt to extend the Schwarzschild solution beyond the horizon, we replace t with t=v-{r}_{\\star }(r). We have\nL216: \nL217:  | dt=dv-d{r}_{\\star }=dv-{\\left(1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\right)}^{-1}dr  |\nL218: \nL219: Making this substitution in the Schwarzschild metric (cite34†6.256 ), we find the new metric\nL220: \nL221:  | d{s}^{2}=-\\left(1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\right)d{v}^{2}+2dvdr+{r}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |  | (6.263)\nL222: This is the Schwarzschild black hole in ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates. We see that the d{r}^{2} terms have now disappeared, and so there is no singularity in the metric at r=2GM. However, the d{v}^{2} term vanishes at r=2GM and, moreover, flips sign for r<2GM. You might worry that this means that the coordinates still go bad there, or even that the signature of the metric changes as we cross the horizon. To allay such worries, we need only compute the determinant of the metric\nL223:  | det(g)=det\\left(\\begin{array}{cccc}\\hfill -(1-\\frac{2GM}{r})\\hfill & \\hfill \\mathrm{\\hspace{0.25em}1}\\hfill & \\hfill \\mathrm{\\hspace{0.25em}\\hspace{0.25em}0}\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 1\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill {r}^{2}\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill {r}^{2}{\\mathrm{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hfill \\end{array}\\right)=-{r}^{4}{\\mathrm{sin}}^{2}\\theta  |\nL224: We see that the dvdr cross-term stops the metric becoming degenerate at the horizon and the signature remains Lorentzian for all values of r. (The metric is still degenerate at the \\theta =0,\\pi but these are simply the poles of the {𝐒}^{2} and we know how to deal with that.)\nL225: ####\nL226: \nL227: This, then, is the advantage of the ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates: the r coordinate can be continued past the horizon, all the way down to the singularity at r=0.\nL228: ####\nL229: The original Schwarchild metric (cite34†6.256 ) was time independent. Mathematically, this follows from the statement that the metric exhibits a timelike Killing vector K={\\partial }_{t}. This Killing vector also exists in the Eddington-Finkelstein extension, where it is now K={\\partial }_{v}. The novelty is that this Killing vector is no longer everywhere timelike. Instead, it remains timelike outside the horizon where {g}_{vv}<0, but becomes spacelike inside the horizon where {g}_{vv}>0.\nL230: In other words, the full black hole geometry is not time independent! We’ll learn more about this feature as we progress.\nL231: ### The Finkelstein Diagram\nL232: \nL233: To build further intuition for the geometry, we can look at the behaviour of light rays coming out of the black hole. These follow paths given by\nL234: \nL235:  | u=t-{r}_{\\star }=\\mathrm{constant}  |\nL236: Eliminating t, in preference of the null coordinate v=t+{r}_{\\star }, outgoing null geodesics satisfy v=2{r}_{\\star }+\\mathrm{constant}. The solutions to this equation have a different nature depending on whether we are outside or inside the horizon. For r>2GM, we can use the original definition (cite40†6.262 ) of the tortoise coordinate {r}_{\\star } to get\nL237: \nL238:  | v=2r+4GM\\mathrm{log}\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{r-2GM}{2GM}}\\right)+\\mathrm{constant}  |\nL239: Clearly the \\mathrm{log} term goes bad for r<2GM. However, it is straightforward to write down a tortoise coordinate that obeys (cite41†6.261 ) on either side of the horizon: we simply need to take the modulus of the argument\nL240: \nL241:  | {r}_{\\star }=r+2GM\\mathrm{log}\\left|{\\displaystyle \\frac{r-2GM}{2GM}}\\right|  |\nL242: This means that {r}_{\\star } is multi-valued: it sits in the range {r}_{\\star }\\in (-\\mathrm{\\infty },+\\mathrm{\\infty }) outside the horizon, and in the range {r}_{\\star }\\in (-\\mathrm{\\infty },0) inside the horizon, with the singularity at {r}_{\\star }=0. Outgoing geodesics inside the horizon then obey\nL243: \nL244:  | v=2r+4GM\\mathrm{log}\\left({\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM-r}{2GM}}\\right)+\\mathrm{constant}  |  | (6.264)\nL245: It remains to find the outgoing null geodesic at the horizon r=2GM. Here the d{v}^{2} term in the metric (cite42†6.263 ) vanishes, and one can check that the surface r=2GM is itself a null geodesic. This agrees with our expectation from Section cite9†6.1.2 where we saw that the horizon is a null surface.\nL246: \nL247: Figure 44: The Finkelstein diagram in ingoing coordinates. Ingoing null geodesics and shown in red, outgoing in blue. Inside the horizon at r=2GM, outgoing geodesics do not go out.\nL248: ####\nL249: \nL250: We can capture this information in a Finkelstein diagram. This is designed so that ingoing null rays travel at 45 degrees. This is simple to do if we label the coordinates of the diagram by t and {r}_{\\star }. However, as we’ve seen, {r}_{\\star } isn’t single valued everywhere in the black hole. For this reason, we will label the spatial coordinate by the original r. We then define a new temporal coordinate {t}_{\\star } by the requirement\nL251: \nL252:  | v=t+{r}_{\\star }={t}_{\\star }+r  |\nL253: So ingoing null rays travel at 45 degrees in the ({t}_{\\star },r) plane, where {t}_{\\star }=v-r. These are shown as the red lines in Figure cite43†44 . Meanwhile, the outgoing null geodesics are shown in blue. Now we can clearly see how the behaviour changes depending on whether the geodesics are inside or outside the horizon. The outgoing geodesics that sit outside the horizon do what their name suggests: they move out.\nL254: In particular, as t\\to \\mathrm{\\infty } (so {t}_{\\star }\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }), the geodesics escape to r\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }.\nL255: ####\nL256: \nL257: The outgoing geodesics that sit inside the horizon are not so lucky. Now as t increases, the geodesics described by (cite44†6.264 ) don’t go “out” at all: instead the “outgoing” light rays move inexorably towards the curvature singularity at r=0. Each of them hits the singularity at some finite {t}_{\\star }.\nL258: \nL259: ####\nL260: \nL261: Bounding these two regions are the null geodesics which simply run along the horizon r=2GM: this is the vertical blue line in the figure.\nL262: ####\nL263: We can also draw light-cones on the Finkelstein diagram. These are the regions bounded by the ingoing and outgoing, future-pointing null geodesics, as shown in the figure. Any massive particle must follow a timelike path, and hence its trajectory must sit within these lightcones. We see immediately one of the key features of black holes: if you venture into past the horizon, you’re not getting back out again. This is forbidden by the causal structure of the spacetime.\nL264: The term black hole really refers to the region r<2GM inside the horizon. Any observer who remains outside the horizon can know nothing about what’s happening inside.\nL265: ####\nL266: We can also use the Finkelstein diagram to tell us what an observer will see if they push their friend into a black hole. The hapless companion sails through the horizon, quite possibly without realising anything is wrong. However, any light signals that are sent back take longer and longer to reach an observer sitting at some fixed radial value r>2GM. This means that the actions of the in-falling friend become increasingly slowed down as they approach the horizon.\nL267: In this way, the observer/villain sitting outside continues to see their friend forever, but knows nothing of their action after they cross the horizon. Furthermore, since the light is now emerging from a deeper and deeper gravitational well, it will appear increasingly redshifted to the outside observer.\nL268: ### Outgoing Eddington-Finkelstein Coordinates\nL269: \nL270: There is a different extension of the exterior of the Schwarzschild black hole, in which we replace the time coordinate t with the null coordinate\nL271: \nL272:  | u=t-{r}_{\\star }  |\nL273: \nL274: Recall the surfaces of constant u correspond to outgoing, radial, null geodesics.\nL275: ####\nL276: \nL277: As before, it is straightforward to make this change of variable. We have t=u+{r}_{\\star }, so\nL278: \nL279:  | dt=du+d{r}_{\\star }=du+{\\left(1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\right)}^{-1}dr  |\nL280: \nL281: Making this substitution in the Schwarzschild metric (cite34†6.256 ), we now find the metric\nL282: \nL283:  | d{s}^{2}=-\\left(1-{\\displaystyle \\frac{2GM}{r}}\\right)d{u}^{2}-2dudr+{r}^{2}d{\\mathrm{\\Omega }}_{2}^{2}  |  | (6.265)\nL284: This is the Schwarzschild solution in outgoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates. The only difference with the ingoing coordinates (cite42†6.263 ) is the sign of the cross-term. However, as we now explain, this seemingly trivial difference greatly changes the interpretation of the metric.\nL285: \nL286: Figure 45: The Finkelstein diagram in outgoing coordinates. Ingoing null geodesics are shown in red, outgoing in blue. Inside the horizon at r=2GM, ingoing geodesics do not go in.\nL287: ####\nL288: \nL289: Once again, the metric is smooth (and non-degenerate) at the horizon so we can happily continue the metric down to the singularity at r=0. However, the region r<2GM now describes a different part of spacetime from the analogous region in ingoing Eddington-Finkelstein coordinates!\nL290: ####\nL291: \nL292: To see this, we can again look at the ingoing and outgoing geodesics, as seen in the Finkelstein diagram in Figure cite45†45 . This time, we pick coordinates so that the outgoing geodesics travel at 45 degrees. This means that we take r and {t}_{\\star }=u+r. The outgoing geodesics are drawn in red as before. But this time we see that they do what their name suggests: they go always go out, regardless of whether they start life behind the horizon.\nL293: ####\nL294: \nL295: This time, it is the ingoing null geodesics that have the interesting property. Those that start life outside are unable to reach the singularity. Instead, they pile up at the horizon. Those that start life behind the horizon have an even stranger property: the ingoing geodesics do not go in. Instead they too move towards the horizon, again unable to cross it.\nL296: ####\nL297: \nL298: We can also ask what becomes of massive particles that sit inside the horizon. As before, their trajectories must lie within future-pointing light cones. We see that they cannot linger inside the horizon for long. The causal structure of the spacetime ultimately ejects them into the region outside the horizon.\nL299: cite46†Image: Ingoing coordinates cover this part of Rindler space. Figure 46: Ingoing coordinates cover this part of Rindler space.  | cite47†Image: Outgoing coordinates cover this part. Figure 47: Outgoing coordinates cover this part.\nL300: ####\nL301: \nL302: This is clearly very different physics from a black hole. Instead, the solution (cite48†6.265 ) is that of a white hole, an object which expels any matter inside. This is the time reversal of a black hole, a fact which can be traced to the relative minus sign between the two metrics (cite42†6.263 ) and (cite48†6.265 ). This time reversal is also manifest in the diagrams: turn the white hole of Figure cite45†45 upside down and you get the black hole of Figure cite43†44 .\n"
